× all 8 alignments, plus randomized | the `data` chunk's length is copied verbatim into `info->size` as a `u64` (signed→unsigned reinterpretation) | identical `info->size` bits | `err15_chunk_size_extremes`, `cfg13_data_chunk_size_extremes` | [x] |","+| 16 | misaligned `data` pointer, offsets 0..7 | the C casts the buffer to `struct caf_*` with no alignment guarantee, so the Rust must use unaligned loads | identical results, no fault | `err16_misaligned_pointer`, `cfg16_misaligned_buffer` | [x] |","+","+## Divergence found and fixed during Phase C","+","+`err06` / `err07` initially **failed in the `dev` profile only**: the C child died","+with `SIGSEGV` (11) while the Rust child died with `SIGABRT` (6).","+","+Cause: rustc's optional Undefined-Behaviour instrumentation (the MIR null-check","+pass, plus the `assert_unsafe_precondition!` guards inside","+`core::ptr::read_unaligned`) is compiled in whenever `debug-assertions` is","+enabled, and it converts precisely the NULL loads/stores of rows 6 and 7 into a","+non-unwinding panic — `SIGABRT` instead of the C library's `SIGSEGV`.  The C","+library is built by CMake with no such instrumentation.","+","+Fix (in `translation/Cargo.toml`, with a matching comment in `src/lib.rs`):","+","+```toml","+[profile.dev]","+debug-assertions = false","+overflow-checks = false","+```","+","+This is load-bearing and is itself under test: re-enabling `debug-assertions`","+makes `err06_null_data_segv` and `err07_null_info_segv` fail again (verified).","+","+## Result","+","+```","+$ cargo test --release --test phase_c_errors","+test result: ok. 17 passed; 0 failed; 1 ignored   (the ignored one is the child-side `crash_worker`)","+```","+","+Every row above is checked off, under both the `dev` and `release` profiles and","+under all three Cargo feature configurations (see `verify.sh`)."]}],"originalFile":"# ERRORS.md — Phase C error-surface table\n\nMechanically derived by grepping `c_src/src/lib.c` for **every** rejection /\nfault path. There are no `assert`s, no `RETURN_ERROR` macros, no error enums and\nno NULL checks anywhere in the C source; the complete set of rejections is:\n\n```\n$ grep -n 'return\\|assert\\|NULL' c_src/src/lib.c\n78:    const struct caf_audio_description *desc = NULL;\n79:    const struct caf_packet_table *pakt = NULL;\n80:    const struct ima_block *blocks = NULL;\n91:        return -1;\n93:        return -2;\n122:        return -3;\n130:    return 0;\n```\n\nThree explicit error returns (`-1`, `-2`, `-3`), one success (`0`), plus the\nimplicit fault paths created by the *absence* of NULL/bounds checking (rows 4-9)\nand by the unguarded `for (;;)` chunk scan (rows 8-9). Rows 4-9 are real inputs\nthat the C library \"handles\" by crashing / diverging, so the Rust must crash /\ndiverge identically — they are tested by re-exec'ing the test binary in a child\nprocess and comparing the termination signal (or the timeout).\n\n| #  | function | trigger (the exact invalid input/condition) | expected C result | test |\n|----|----------|---------------------------------------------|-------------------|------|\n| 1  | `ima_parse` | `ima_btoh32(header->type) != 'ffac'` — the first 4 bytes of `data` are not ASCII `\"caff\"` (checked *before* anything else, so version/chunks are irrelevant) | `return -1` | `err01_bad_magic` |\n| 2  | `ima_parse` | magic OK **and** `ima_btoh16(header->version) != 1` — bytes 4..6 are not big-endian `0x0001` | `return -2` | `err02_bad_version` |\n| 3  | `ima_parse` | magic + version OK, a `desc` chunk was seen before the `data` chunk, but `ima_btoh32(desc->format_id) != '4ami'` — `desc+8..12` is not ASCII `\"ima4\"`. Returns *before* `pakt` is dereferenced, so this path is safe even when `pakt == NULL`. | `return -3` | `err03_bad_format_id`, `err03_bad_format_id_no_pakt` |\n| 4  | `ima_parse` | magic + version OK and the `data` chunk is reached with **no preceding `desc` chunk** ⇒ `desc == NULL` ⇒ `desc->format_id` reads address `0x8` | SIGSEGV (NULL deref, read) | `err04_null_desc_segv` |\n| 5  | `ima_parse` | magic + version + `format_id` OK and the `data` chunk is reached with **no preceding `pakt` chunk** ⇒ `pakt == NULL` ⇒ `pakt->frame_count` reads address `0x8` | SIGSEGV (NULL deref, read) | `err05_null_pakt_segv` |\n| 6  | `ima_parse` | `data == NULL` (no null check on the buffer) ⇒ `header->type` reads address `0x0` | SIGSEGV (NULL deref, read) | `err06_null_data_segv` |\n| 7  | `ima_parse` | `info == NULL` with an otherwise fully valid buffer (no null check on the out-param) ⇒ `info->blocks = ...` writes address `0x0` | SIGSEGV (NULL deref, write) | `err07_null_info_segv` |\n| 8  | `ima_parse` | magic + version OK but the buffer contains **no `data` chunk** — the `for (;;)` scan has no bound and walks off the end of the allocation | SIGSEGV (out-of-bounds read) | `err08_no_data_chunk_segv` |\n| 9  | `ima_parse` | a non-`data` chunk whose `size == -16` ⇒ `chunk = &chunk[1] + (-16) == chunk` ⇒ the scan never advances | infinite loop (never returns) | `err09_self_referential_chunk_hangs` |\n| 10 | `ima_parse` | zero-length / truncated buffer (fewer than 8 bytes, e.g. 0..7 bytes) mapped so that reading past it faults ⇒ the header read itself is out of bounds | SIGSEGV (out-of-bounds read) | `err10_truncated_header_segv` |\n\n## Generic FFI boundary cases (covered even though not distinct C branches)\n\n| #  | case | why it is exercised | expected | test |\n|----|------|---------------------|----------|------|\n| 11 | `version` one step past the valid range: `0`, `2`, `0xFFFF`, and every other of the 65 535 invalid values (exhaustive) | `!= 1` is the only accepted value | `-2` for all but `1` | `err11_version_exhaustive` |\n| 12 | `magic` one step past valid: `\"cafe\"`, `\"cafg\"`, `\"baff\"`, `\"CAFF\"`, byte-swapped `\"ffac\"`, plus 20 000 random `u32`s | only `\"caff\"` accepted | `-1` unless exactly `\"caff\"` | `err12_magic_randomized` |\n| 13 | `format_id` one step past valid: `\"ima3\"`, `\"ima5\"`, `\"IMA4\"`, `\"4ami\"`, plus 20 000 random `u32`s | only `\"ima4\"` accepted | `-3` unless exactly `\"ima4\"` | `err13_format_id_randomized` |\n| 14 | out-of-range \"enum\" values across the FFI boundary: `chunk->type` is an unconstrained `ima_u32_t` used in an `if`/`else if` chain with 3 recognised values — 20 000 random 32-bit values with no valid variant must all take the fall-through (skip) branch | C `switch`/`if` chains accept any `int`; an unrecognised chunk type must be skipped, not mis-dispatched | identical skip behaviour, final `0`/`-3` matches | `err14_unknown_chunk_type_enum_fuzz` |\n| 15 | oversized / negative lengths: `chunk->size` = `0`, `±1`, `i64::MIN`, `i64::MAX`, `u64::MAX`, `-16`(row 9), `-32`, and random values — the `data` chunk's size is copied verbatim into `info->size` as a `u64` | signed→unsigned reinterpretation of an out-of-range length | identical `info->size` bits | `err15_chunk_size_extremes`, `cfg_*` |\n| 16 | misaligned `data` pointer (offsets 1..7 into the allocation) — C reads `u32`/`u64`/`f64` through casts with no alignment guarantee | Rust must use `read_unaligned`, not `read` | identical results, no fault | `cfg16_misaligned_buffer` |\n","userModified":false}}
